WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.229
codebase-video: a repository in, a narrated teaching video out.

00:00:06.429 --> 00:00:15.624
Here is the goal: after this video, you will be able to say what the plugin makes, how the video is checked against the code, and how to install it.

00:00:18.024 --> 00:00:23.848
So we go in three steps: what it makes, then the checking, and finally running and installing it.

00:00:26.848 --> 00:00:28.683
First, what you get at the end.

00:00:29.783 --> 00:00:37.761
codebase-video is a Claude Code plugin that turns a repository into a narrated, animated video that teaches it.

00:00:38.061 --> 00:00:44.696
That means a voice explains how the code works, while diagrams and the real source code appear on screen.

00:00:46.496 --> 00:00:52.512
For example, you point it at your own codebase to onboard a teammate, or at someone else's to learn it.

00:00:55.112 --> 00:01:03.987
Here are frames from a real 14:40 video of the public FCQRS repository, made with this plugin.

00:01:05.787 --> 00:01:10.459
For example, this is one of its flow diagrams, with labels on its arrows.

00:01:11.759 --> 00:01:18.500
Next, one example with real values: a balance of 100, and a command, Withdraw 30.

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.213
And here is that repository's real code on screen.

00:01:27.313 --> 00:01:31.857
To recap: a repository goes in, and a teaching video comes out.

00:01:32.157 --> 00:01:35.763
A voice explains while diagrams and real code appear.

00:01:36.063 --> 00:01:38.452
And those frames come from a real video.

00:01:42.452 --> 00:01:46.911
But a video repeats every mistake in its script, so next comes the checking.

00:01:47.811 --> 00:01:53.656
The work is done by agents, and an agent is a separate Claude Code worker given one job.

00:01:53.956 --> 00:01:59.481
First, one agent reads the code, file by file, and writes the teaching document.

00:01:59.781 --> 00:02:04.368
In that document, every factual sentence cites a file and its lines.

00:02:06.168 --> 00:02:14.915
Then a fresh agent, the verifier, which did not write the document, checks every citation and code block against the repository.

00:02:16.115 --> 00:02:17.651
Next comes the script.

00:02:17.951 --> 00:02:24.543
It may not say anything the document does not say, and a fresh agent audits it before any visuals are built.

00:02:26.343 --> 00:02:32.209
After that, agents draw the scenes in parallel, and fresh agents audit what is on screen.

00:02:32.509 --> 00:02:38.312
Finally, a scan checks a frame every 0.25 s for overlaps and empty screens.

00:02:40.112 --> 00:02:46.491
The tricky part is that the scan cannot judge meaning, so an agent looks at a still for every finding.

00:02:46.791 --> 00:02:50.673
That is a lot of reviewers, and not one of them asks for a meeting.

00:02:52.073 --> 00:02:57.236
To recap: one agent writes a cited document, and a fresh agent checks it.

00:02:57.536 --> 00:03:00.181
The script says only what that document says.

00:03:00.481 --> 00:03:02.913
And the visuals are audited, then scanned.

00:03:06.913 --> 00:03:10.839
Finally, where this runs, what it costs, and how to install it.

00:03:11.939 --> 00:03:18.957
Claude Code reads your code as in any session, but the video is made on your computer, and nothing is uploaded.

00:03:20.757 --> 00:03:31.488
That works because the voice is Kokoro, a local text-to-speech voice, your installed Chrome renders every frame, and ffmpeg encodes the result.

00:03:31.788 --> 00:03:39.169
Be careful, though: the first run does download the voice and a browser driver, about 800 MB, once.

00:03:40.369 --> 00:03:41.628
Next, the lengths.

00:03:41.928 --> 00:03:49.800
A short video is 3-5 minutes, a tour, the default, is 6-10, and a deep video is 20-28.

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.805
The README gives rough counts of agent tasks: about 10, 12 and 30.

00:03:57.105 --> 00:04:00.796
And more tasks means more time and more of your Claude usage.

00:04:01.996 --> 00:04:07.927
You need Node.js 18 or later, ffmpeg, and Google Chrome or Chromium.

00:04:08.227 --> 00:04:17.101
The plugin's engine is written in F# but ships compiled to JavaScript, so that is the whole list: no Python and no .NET.

00:04:17.401 --> 00:04:28.729
Next, install with two commands: /plugin marketplace add OnurGumus/codebase-video, and /plugin install codebase-video@codebase-video.

00:04:30.529 --> 00:04:35.457
After that, run /codebase-video in the repository you want to teach.

00:04:37.057 --> 00:04:41.431
To recap: the video is made on your computer, and nothing is uploaded.

00:04:41.731 --> 00:04:44.909
More agent tasks means more of your Claude usage.

00:04:45.209 --> 00:04:49.369
And you install with two commands, then run /codebase-video.

00:04:52.969 --> 00:04:57.919
So now you can say what the plugin makes, how the video is checked, and how to start.

00:04:58.219 --> 00:05:05.301
To try it, install with the two commands you just saw, then run /codebase-video in your own repository.
